<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malaysian Chinese Association</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Malayan Chinese Association, MCA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malaysian Chinese Association, founded in 1949 as the Malayan Chinese Association under Tan Cheng Lock, is a political party representing ethnic Chinese in Malaysia. It began partly as a welfare body for Chinese moved into new villages during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a>. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">United Malays National Organisation</a> in the Alliance that won independence in 1957 and remained a partner in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-nasional">Barisan Nasional</a> coalition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malaysian-chinese-association.jpg" alt="Malaysian Chinese Association" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Great Brightstar (originally Malaysian Chinese Association) · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_Malaysian_Chinese_Association.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Malayan Chinese Association was formed on 27 February 1949 with the implicit support of the British colonial administration, chiefly to manage the welfare of people interned in the New Villages created under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/briggs-plan">Briggs Plan</a>. Its declaration as a political party in 1951 was written by the Straits Chinese businessman Tan Cheng Lock, its first president. Early members were mostly landowners and businessmen, while working people in the New Villages largely joined the Socialist Front. Many leading members, such as Leong Yew Koh and Henry H.S. Lee, were also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> members opposed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-communist-party">Malayan Communist Party</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1952 the party allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">United Malays National Organisation</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a> municipal elections, the start of the Alliance Party, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaysian-indian-congress">Malaysian Indian Congress</a> joined in 1954. In 1955 the Alliance won 51 of 52 seats, the MCA all 15 of its own. Lim Chong Eu, president from 1958, quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman">Tunku Abdul Rahman</a> over seat numbers and resigned. Tan Siew Sin, son of Tan Cheng Lock, led the party from 1961. In 1969, challenged by the new Democratic Action Party and Gerakan, it won only 13 of the 33 seats it contested, and the election was followed by the May 13 Riots.</p><p>When the Alliance widened into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-nasional">Barisan Nasional</a>, the party lost standing beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">UMNO</a>. In 1982 Lee San Choon led it to 24 of its 28 allocated parliamentary seats. The Deposit-Taking Cooperatives begun by Tan Koon Swan ended in a scandal that cost depositors an estimated RM3.6 billion. Ling Liong Sik, president from 1986, rebuilt its finances, founded Universiti Tunku Abdul Rahman in 2001 and won 30 of its 34 allocated parliamentary seats in 1995. Factional quarrels between Team A and Team B followed, and the purchase of Nanyang Siang Pau through Huaren Holdings drew public protests.</p><p>After its losses in 2008, the party won only 7 of the 37 parliamentary seats it contested in 2013 and for a time had no cabinet minister, for the first time since independence, until Liow Tiong Lai led it back into government. In 2018 it kept one parliamentary seat and went into opposition with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-nasional">Barisan Nasional</a>. It returned to government in March 2020 alongside Perikatan Nasional, and after the 2022 election, in which it won two seats, it sat on the government backbench without cabinet posts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malaysian Chinese Association” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Malaya became independent on 31 August 1957 under Tunku Abdul Rahman, leader of the Alliance of the United Malays National Organisation (UMNO), the <b>Malayan Chinese Association</b> and the Malayan Indian Congress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Alliance of UMNO, the <b>Malayan Chinese Association</b> and the Malayan Indian Congress won the 1955 election under Tunku Abdul Rahman, who negotiated independence on 31 August 1957.</blockquote><a href="/state/federation-of-malaya">Federation of Malaya</a></li></ul></section>
